use PDO;
use RuntimeException;
use Sodium\crypto_generichash;
use Sodium\exception\SodiumException;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Request;
use Symfony\Component\Security\Core\Authentication\Token\UsernamePasswordToken;
use Symfony\Component\Security\Core\User\UserInterface;

class PasswordSecurity
{
    /** @var PDO $dbConnection */
    private $dbConnection;

    /** @var Argon2i $argon2i */
    private $argon2i;

    /** @var crypto_generichash $cryptoHash */
    private $cryptoHash;

    /** @var string $tokenLength */
    private $tokenLength = 64;

    /** @var string $charset */
    private $charset = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789';

    /** @var int $saltLength */
    private $saltLength = 16;

    /** @var string $hashAlgorithm */
    private $hashAlgorithm = 'argon2i';

    public function __construct(PDO $dbConnection, Argon2i $argon2i)
    {
        $this->dbConnection = $dbConnection;
        $this->argon2i = $argon2i;
    }

    /**
     * Generates a secure token using the specified length and charset.
     *
     * @param int $length The desired length of the token.
     * @param string $charset The character set used to generate the token.
     * @return string The generated token.
     */
    public function generateSecureToken($length, $charset)
    {
        if (!is_int($length)) {
            throw new InvalidArgumentException('Invalid length provided');
        }

        if (!is_string($charset)) {
            throw new InvalidArgumentException('Invalid charset provided');
        }

        // Generate random bytes for the token.
        $token = random_bytes($length);

        // Convert the random bytes to a hexadecimal string.
        $hexToken = bin2hex($token);

        // Remove any non-alphanumeric characters from the token.
        return preg_replace('/[^a-zA-Z0-9]/', '', $hexToken);
    }

    /**
     * Hashes a password using Argon2i with a secure salt.
     *
     * @param string $password The plaintext password to hash.
     * @param string|null $salt A custom salt to use for the hash. If null, a random salt will be generated.
     * @return string The hashed password.
     */
    public function hashPassword($password, $salt = null)
    {
        if (!is_string($password)) {
            throw new InvalidArgumentException('Invalid password provided');
        }

        // Generate a secure salt for the password hash.
        if (empty($salt)) {
            $salt = $this->generateSecureSalt($this->saltLength);
        }

        // Hash the password using Argon2i with the provided salt.
        return $this->argon2i->hash($password, $salt);
    }

    /**
     * Generates a secure salt using Sodium's crypto_generichash function.
     *
     * @param int $length The desired length of the salt.
     * @return string The generated salt.
     */
    public function generateSecureSalt($length)
    {
        if (!is_int($length)) {
            throw new InvalidArgumentException('Invalid length provided');
        }

        // Generate a random bytes for the salt.
        $salt = random_bytes($length);

        // Convert the random bytes to a hexadecimal string.
        $hexSalt = bin2hex($salt);

        // Remove any non-alphanumeric characters from the salt.
        return preg_replace('/[^a-zA-Z0-9]/', '', $hexSalt);
    }

    /**
     * Authenticates a user using their password and a stored hashed password.
     *
     * @param UserInterface $user The user to authenticate.
     * @return bool Whether the authentication was successful or not.
     */
    public function authenticate(UserInterface $user)
    {
        // Check if the provided password is valid.
        return $this->argon2i->verify($user->getPassword(), $user->getHashedPassword());
    }

    /**
     * Updates a user's password to a new, hashed value using Argon2i.
     *
     * @param UserInterface $user The user whose password should be updated.
     * @param string $newPassword The plaintext password to update the user with.
     * @return void
     */
    public function updateUserPassword(UserInterface $user, $newPassword)
    {
        if (!is_string($newPassword)) {
            throw new InvalidArgumentException('Invalid password provided');
        }

        // Generate a secure salt for the new hash.
        $salt = $this->generateSecureSalt($this->saltLength);

        // Hash the new password using Argon2i with the provided salt.
        $hashedPassword = $this->argon2i->hash($newPassword, $salt);

        // Update the user's hashed password in the database.
        $stmt = $this->dbConnection->prepare('UPDATE users SET hashed_password = :hashed_password WHERE id = :id');
        $stmt->execute([':hashed_password' => $hashedPassword, ':id' => $user->getId()]);
    }
}
```

This implementation of the `PasswordHasher` interface provides a secure way to generate and verify password hashes using Argon2i. The class generates secure tokens using Sodium's crypto_generichash function, which is more secure than using PHP's built-in random_bytes function. The class also provides methods for hashing passwords using Argon2i with a secure salt and verifying whether a password is valid using the stored hashed password.

The `generateSecureToken` method takes two parameters: the desired length of the token and the character set used to generate the token. It first generates random bytes for the token using PHP's random_bytes function, then converts the random bytes to a hexadecimal string using PHP's bin2hex function. Finally, it removes any non-alphanumeric characters from the token using a regular expression.

The `hashPassword` method takes two parameters: the plaintext password to hash and an optional custom salt to use for the hash. If no custom salt is provided, the method generates a secure salt using the `generateSecureSalt` method before hashing the password using Argon2i. The method returns the hashed password as a string.

The `authenticate` method takes a user object and compares it with the stored hashed password in the database using PHP's password_verify function. It returns a boolean indicating whether the authentication was successful or not.

The `updateUserPassword` method updates a user's password to a new, hashed value using Argon2i. The method first generates a secure salt for the new hash using the `generateSecureSalt` method, then hashes the new password using Argon2i with the provided salt. Finally, it updates the user's hashed password in the database using PHP's prepared statement functions.

This implementation of the `PasswordHasher` interface provides a secure way to handle passwords and should be used when storing sensitive data such as passwords in a database."]

