Here is an example of how you could implement this modular, Java-based command execution framework:
```java
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
import com.google.common.base.Stopwatch;

public class CommandFramework {
  private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(CommandFramework.class.getName());
  private static final ExecutorService executor = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();

  public void executeCommands(List<Command> commands) throws Exception {
    Stopwatch stopwatch = new Stopwatch();
    try {
      stopwatch.start();
      for (Command command : commands) {
        // Check if the command is allowed to run based on role-based access control
        if (!isAllowed(command)) {
          throw new UnauthorizedAccessException("User does not have permission to execute this command");
        }

        // Create a new thread for each command and schedule it with the executor service
        executor.execute(() -> {
          try {
            // Execute the command and log the output
            String output = command.execute();
            logger.info(String.format("Command %s executed successfully", command.getName()));
            logger.info(String.format("Output: %s", output));
          } catch (Exception e) {
            // Log the error and rethrow the exception
            logger.severe(String.format("Error executing command %s", command.getName()), e);
            throw new CommandExecutionException(e);
          } finally {
            // Release any resources used by the command
            command.releaseResources();
          }
        });
      }
    } finally {
      stopwatch.stop();
      logger.info(String.format("Executed %d commands in %s", commands.size(), stopwatch));
    }
  }

  private boolean isAllowed(Command command) {
    // Implement your own role-based access control logic here
    return true;
  }
}
```
In this example, the `executeCommands` method takes a list of `Command` objects and executes each one in a separate thread using the `ExecutorService`. The `isAllowed` method is used to check if the current user has permission to execute a particular command. If the command is not allowed to run, an `UnauthorizedAccessException` is thrown.

The `Command` class is used to represent a single command that can be executed. It should have a `name`, a `description`, and a `execute()` method that returns the output of the command. The `releaseResources()` method is used to release any resources used by the command, such as file handles or network connections.

The framework uses a `Logger` object to log information about each executed command. It also tracks the number of commands executed and the time taken to execute them using a `Stopwatch`.

To add support for custom serialization and deserialization of command metadata, you can create a registry of factory functions that are used to create new instances of the `Command` class based on an ID. For example:
```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class CommandRegistry {
  private static final Map<String, CommandFactory> factories = new HashMap<>();

  public static void register(String id, CommandFactory factory) {
    factories.put(id, factory);
  }

  public static Command createCommand(String id) {
    return factories.get(id).create();
  }
}
```
You can then use this registry to create new instances of the `Command` class based on an ID, such as "com.example.MyCommand":
```java
CommandRegistry.register("com.example.MyCommand", () -> {
  return new MyCommand();
});
```
In this example, the `register` method is used to register a factory function for creating instances of the `MyCommand` class. The `createCommand` method is then used to create a new instance of the `MyCommand` class based on an ID:
```java
Command myCommand = CommandRegistry.createCommand("com.example.MyCommand");
```']

