// Custom deserialization strategy that accommodates object versioning and backward compatibility
class Deserializer {
  private static final Map<Byte, Class<?>> SIGNATURE_TO_CLASS = new HashMap<>();

  // Initialize the signature to class mapping with known object types
  static {
    SIGNATURE_TO_CLASS.put((byte) 'A', MyObject1.class);
    SIGNATURE_TO_CLASS.put((byte) 'B', MyObject2.class);
    SIGNATURE_TO_CLASS.put((byte) 'C', MyObject3.class);
  }

  // Dynamic deserialization method that supports multiple object types
  public static Object deserialize(byte[] bytes) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
    ByteArrayInputStream bais = new ByteArrayInputStream(bytes);
    DataInputStream dis = new DataInputStream(bais);
    byte signature = dis.readByte();

    // Handle unknown signatures by defaulting to a fallback object type
    if (!SIGNATURE_TO_CLASS.containsKey(signature)) {
      return MyObject4.class;
    }

    Class<?> clazz = SIGNATURE_TO_CLASS.get(signature);
    ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(dis);
    Object obj = ois.readObject();

    // Perform version checks and backward compatibility updates if necessary
    if (obj instanceof MyObject1) {
      MyObject1 myObj1 = (MyObject1) obj;
      // Update myObj1's fields as needed for newer versions
    } else if (obj instanceof MyObject2) {
      MyObject2 myObj2 = (MyObject2) obj;
      // Update myObj2's fields as needed for newer versions
    } else if (obj instanceof MyObject3) {
      MyObject3 myObj3 = (MyObject3) obj;
      // Update myObj3's fields as needed for newer versions
    }

    return obj;
  }
}
```
This code uses the `DataInputStream` and `ObjectInputStream` classes to deserialize a byte array containing multiple objects, each prefixed with a signature byte. The `SIGNATURE_TO_CLASS` map is used to associate each signature byte with its corresponding class type. If an unknown signature byte is encountered, the method defaults to using a fallback object type.

The deserialization process performs version checks and backward compatibility updates if necessary by casting the deserialized object to its specific type and updating its fields as needed for newer versions. The `MyObject4` class is used as a fallback object in case of unknown signatures or unexpected errors during deserialization.

The method returns the deserialized object, which can be used by the service's processing logic.

Please note that this code is just an example and may need to be adjusted according to the specific requirements and constraints of your project. It is important to carefully consider the trade-offs between efficiency, usability, maintainability, and scalability in designing a robust deserialization strategy for your service."]

