To implement a scalable video processing pipeline in C that integrates with third-party transcoding services, we will need to consider several engineering challenges, such as system integration, performance optimization, resource management, error handling, concurrency and scalability, and usability and interface.

Here are some potential solutions for each of these challenges:

1. System Integration: To ensure seamless integration with third-party services, we can use a load balancer to distribute incoming requests across multiple instances of the transcoding service. We can also implement failover and fallback mechanisms to handle interruptions or network latency.
2. Performance Optimization: To optimize performance, we can use techniques such as caching, prefetching, and parallel processing to reduce the time required to process video files. We can also employ adaptive load balancing strategies that dynamically adjust the number of instances based on real-time performance metrics, cost, and availability.
3. Resource Management: To minimize resource usage while maintaining service reliability, we can use techniques such as resource pooling and containerization to allocate resources efficiently. We can also implement retry logic with an adaptive exponential backoff strategy to handle failed connections or data retrieval issues.
4. Error Handling: To implement comprehensive error handling and logging mechanisms, we can use a centralized error reporting system that collects errors from different components of the pipeline and provides actionable insights for troubleshooting. We can also implement fallback mechanisms to handle specific types of errors or failures.
5. Concurrency and Scalability: To design the system to handle high concurrency and varying workloads, we can use a distributed architecture that scales horizontally by adding more instances of the transcoding service. We can also employ techniques such as message queuing and task scheduling to manage incoming requests and ensure that they are processed efficiently.
6. Usability and Interface: To ensure usability and interface, we can provide a simple and intuitive user interface that allows users to easily configure and monitor the pipeline. We can also implement automated testing and validation processes to ensure that the system is functioning correctly and providing reliable performance.

Here's an example implementation of these solutions in C:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <netinet/in.h>
#include <arpa/inet.h>
#include <netdb.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>

// Define the transcoding service URL and port
const char* TRANSCODING_URL = "https://transcoding-service.com";
int TRANSCODING_PORT = 80;

// Define the load balancer URL and port
const char* LOADBALANCER_URL = "http://loadbalancer.com";
int LOADBALANCER_PORT = 80;

// Define the resource pool size and concurrency limit
const int POOL_SIZE = 10;
const int CONCURRENCY_LIMIT = 5;

// Define the retry count and interval
const int RETRY_COUNT = 3;
const int RETRY_INTERVAL = 5;

// Define the error reporting system URL and port
const char* ERROR_REPORTING_URL = "https://error-reporting.com";
int ERROR_REPORTING_PORT = 80;

// Define the user interface URL and port
const char* USER_INTERFACE_URL = "http://user-interface.com";
int USER_INTERFACE_PORT = 80;

// Function to handle transcoding requests
void transcode(char* file_path, int format) {
    // Check if the file path is valid
    if (!file_path || strlen(file_path) == 0) {
        return;
    }

    // Set up a socket to connect to the transcoding service
    struct sockaddr_in server_address;
    int client_socket = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
    if (client_socket < 0) {
        perror("socket");
        return;
    }

    // Set up the socket address to connect to the transcoding service
    server_address.sin_family = AF_INET;
    server_address.sin_port = htons(TRANSCODING_PORT);
    if (inet_pton(AF_INET, TRANSCODING_URL, &server_address.sin_addr) <= 0) {
        perror("inet_pton");
        return;
    }

    // Connect to the transcoding service
    int status = connect(client_socket, (struct sockaddr*)&server_address, sizeof(server_address));
    if (status < 0) {
        perror("connect");
        return;
    }

    // Send a request to the transcoding service to transcode the file
    char* request = "GET /transcode HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: transcoding-service.com\r\nAccept: */*\r\nContent-Length: 0\r\n\r\n";
    send(client_socket, request, strlen(request), 0);

    // Receive the response from the transcoding service
    char* response = malloc(1024 * 1024);
    memset(response, 0, 1024 * 1024);
    int bytes_received = recv(client_socket, response, 1024 * 1024, 0);
    if (bytes_received <= 0) {
        perror("recv");
        return;
    }

    // Close the socket connection to the transcoding service
    close(client_socket);

    // Parse the response from the transcoding service
    char* content = strstr(response, "Content-Type: ");
    if (!content) {
        printf("Invalid response from transcoding service\n");
        return;
    }
    content += 14; // Move past "Content-Type: "

    // Write the transcoded file to disk
    char* output_file = malloc(strlen(file_path) + strlen(".") + 2);
    sprintf(output_file, "%s.%d", file_path, format);
    FILE* out = fopen(output_file, "wb");
    if (!out) {
        perror("fopen");
        return;
    }
    fwrite(content, 1, bytes_received - (content - response), out);
    fclose(out);
}

// Function to handle error reporting requests
