To support multi-factor authentication (MFA), you could include an additional layer of verification for users who have enabled it in their account settings. This could involve requesting a second form of verification, such as a fingerprint scan or a one-time code sent via SMS, before granting access to the protected resource.

To handle high availability and resilience, you could implement the service using a distributed architecture that allows multiple instances to share the session data. This would ensure that even if one instance fails or becomes unavailable, another instance can take over and continue serving requests without disrupting the user experience.

