This code is designed to be as modular and reusable as possible, with most of the logic encapsulated in separate classes such as `GameLogic`, `GameStateManager`, `SpatialHash`, and `AttackManager`. The `GameLogic` class is responsible for initializing and updating the game state manager and spatial hash, while the other classes are responsible for specific aspects of the game logic.

The `executeDynamicAttackSequence` function in the `GameStateManager` class is where we handle adding/removing attacks from the sequence, as well as switching between different types of attacks. This function first checks if there are enough attacks to switch to a new type, and if so it removes the existing attacks before adding the new ones. We also check if the entity is already attacking this type of attack, and if so we do not add more attacks to the sequence.

In the `main` function, we create an instance of the `GameLogic` class and call its `initialize` function to initialize the game state manager and spatial hash. We then test the dynamic attack sequence code by calling the `executeDynamicAttackSequence` function with a specific entity ID, attack types, and number of attacks.

This is just one possible way to implement dynamic attack sequences in your game, but you can adjust the code as needed to fit your specific requirements. Good luck!']

