Here is a possible implementation of the Java function that takes an integer array as input and returns the number of disjoint pairs of elements in the array, using a Union-Find data structure to efficiently check for disjointness:
```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class DisjointPairs {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = new int[] {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        int numDisjointPairs = findNumDisjointPairs(arr);
        long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        System.out.println("Number of disjoint pairs: " + numDisjointPairs);
        System.out.println("Time taken: " + (endTime - startTime) + " milliseconds");
    }

    private static int findNumDisjointPairs(int[] arr) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> parent = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i : arr) {
            if (!parent.containsKey(i)) {
                parent.put(i, i);
            }
        }
        int numDisjointPairs = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
            int rootI = findRoot(parent, i);
            for (int j = i + 1; j < arr.length; j++) {
                int rootJ = findRoot(parent, j);
                if (rootI != rootJ) {
                    parent.put(rootI, rootJ);
                    numDisjointPairs++;
                }
            }
        }
        return numDisjointPairs;
    }

    private static int findRoot(Map<Integer, Integer> parent, int i) {
        while (parent.containsKey(i)) {
            i = parent.get(i);
        }
        return i;
    }
}
```
In this implementation, we use a HashMap to keep track of the parents of each element in the array. We start by iterating through the elements of the array and creating a map from each element to its root (itself if it is a root). Then, we iterate through the pairs of elements and check if they are disjoint. If they are not disjoint, we update the parent map to point to the same root for both elements. We keep track of the number of disjoint pairs and return it at the end.

The time complexity of this implementation is O(n) where n is the length of the input array, since we only need to iterate through the elements once to find all the roots. The space complexity is O(n) as well, since we need to keep track of the parents for each element in the map.

Note that this solution assumes that the input array has no duplicate elements. If there are duplicate elements, the time and space complexities will increase accordingly.']

