The system also implements backpressure mechanisms to manage bursts of transactions and ensure uninterrupted processing of valid transactions. The system uses a message queue to decouple transaction ingestion from processing and implements a load balancing strategy to distribute workloads evenly across multiple instances or nodes.

The system optimizes memory usage to prevent excessive heap consumption, especially during high-throughput periods. The system also implements garbage collection tuning and minimizes temporary object creation. If necessary, the system can use off-heap memory by providing configuration options to dynamically adjust memory allocation based on available resources.

The system enhances the security of transactions that require enhanced privacy protection by implementing encryption and secure data storage mechanisms. The system also implements a mechanism for handling a subset of transactions that require special handling, ensuring that no transaction data is exposed to unauthorized users or systems.

The system handles new dependencies introduced by the introduction of new types of transactions by providing additional logic to ensure that only compatible transactions can proceed. The system also implements robust error handling for scenarios where the time-stamping service is unavailable or returns inconsistent data, logging all interactions with the service and notifying administrators in case of critical failures.

