#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <cmath>

using namespace std;

class TCPConnection {
public:
    int id;
    string ip;
    bool is_active;
};

class ConnectionManager {
private:
    vector<TCPConnection> connections;
    map<int, int> connection_map; // maps connection id to index in connections vector
    int next_id;

public:
    ConnectionManager() {
        next_id = 0;
    }

    void add_connection(string ip) {
        TCPConnection conn;
        conn.id = next_id++;
        conn.ip = ip;
        conn.is_active = true;
        connections.push_back(conn);
        connection_map[conn.id] = connections.size() - 1;
    }

    void remove_connection(int id) {
        int index = connection_map[id];
        TCPConnection conn = connections[index];
        connections.erase(connections.begin() + index);
        connection_map.erase(conn.id);
    }

    bool is_active(int id) {
        return connections[connection_map[id]].is_active;
    }

    void set_inactive(int id) {
        connections[connection_map[id]].is_active = false;
    }
};
```
This code defines a `TCPConnection` class that represents a single TCP connection and its associated metadata. It also defines a `ConnectionManager` class that manages a pool of TCP connections, including adding, removing, and marking connections as inactive or active.

The `ConnectionManager` class has the following members:

* `connections`: A vector of `TCPConnection` objects representing all the connections in the pool.
* `connection_map`: A map that maps connection IDs to their indices in the `connections` vector. This allows for efficient lookups and updates of individual connections.
* `next_id`: An integer variable that keeps track of the next available connection ID when adding new connections.

The `add_connection()` method adds a new TCP connection to the pool, assigning it a unique ID and initializing its metadata. The `remove_connection()` method removes a connection from the pool by erasing it from the `connections` vector and updating the `connection_map`.

The `is_active()` method returns whether a connection is currently active (i.e., not marked as inactive) based on its ID. The `set_inactive()` method marks a connection as inactive by setting its `is_active` flag to `false`.

The `ConnectionManager` class also includes several methods for managing the pool of connections, such as adding and removing connections, marking them as active or inactive, and querying their status. These methods are designed to be efficient and scalable, with a focus on minimizing memory usage and reducing CPU overhead.']

