Here is a sample implementation of the scalable, multi-tenant data processing platform in Java:
```java
public class DataProcessingPlatform {
  private final Map<String, Tenant> tenants;
  private final List<DataIngestionComponent> ingestionComponents;
  private final List<DataProcessingComponent> processingComponents;
  private final List<DataStorageComponent> storageComponents;

  public DataProcessingPlatform(Map<String, Tenant> tenants) {
    this.tenants = tenants;
    this.ingestionComponents = new ArrayList<>();
    this.processingComponents = new ArrayList<>();
    this.storageComponents = new ArrayList<>();
  }

  public void addTenant(Tenant tenant) {
    tenants.put(tenant.getName(), tenant);
  }

  public void removeTenant(String tenantName) {
    tenants.remove(tenantName);
  }

  public void startIngestion() {
    for (DataIngestionComponent component : ingestionComponents) {
      component.start();
    }
  }

  public void stopIngestion() {
    for (DataIngestionComponent component : ingestionComponents) {
      component.stop();
    }
  }

  public void startProcessing() {
    for (DataProcessingComponent component : processingComponents) {
      component.start();
    }
  }

  public void stopProcessing() {
    for (DataProcessingComponent component : processingComponents) {
      component.stop();
    }
  }

  public void startStorage() {
    for (DataStorageComponent component : storageComponents) {
      component.start();
    }
  }

  public void stopStorage() {
    for (DataStorageComponent component : storageComponents) {
      component.stop();
    }
  }

  public void addIngestionComponent(DataIngestionComponent component) {
    ingestionComponents.add(component);
  }

  public void removeIngestionComponent(DataIngestionComponent component) {
    ingestionComponents.remove(component);
  }

  public void addProcessingComponent(DataProcessingComponent component) {
    processingComponents.add(component);
  }

  public void removeProcessingComponent(DataProcessingComponent component) {
    processingComponents.remove(component);
  }

  public void addStorageComponent(DataStorageComponent component) {
    storageComponents.add(component);
  }

  public void removeStorageComponent(DataStorageComponent component) {
    storageComponents.remove(component);
  }
}
```
The `DataProcessingPlatform` class is the main entry point for the data processing platform, and it manages the ingestion, processing, and storage of data from multiple tenants. The platform supports dynamic addition and removal of tenants, ingestion components, processing components, and storage components.

The `Tenant` class represents a single tenant in the system, with their own set of data ingestion, processing, and storage configurations. Each tenant can define custom data processing workflows, which are dynamically adjusted based on real-time system load and predefined service-level agreements (SLAs).

The `DataIngestionComponent` class represents a component responsible for ingesting data from various sources into the platform. The component can be implemented using a variety of technologies, such as Apache Kafka, Apache Flume, or AWS Kinesis.

The `DataProcessingComponent` class represents a component responsible for processing and transforming the data before storing it in the distributed data warehouse. The component can be implemented using a variety of technologies, such as Apache Beam, Apache Flink, or Apache Spark.

The `DataStorageComponent` class represents a component responsible for storing the processed data in a distributed data warehouse. The component can be implemented using a variety of technologies, such as Apache Hadoop, Apache Cassandra, or AWS S3.

To optimize performance and resource utilization, the platform can incorporate techniques such as batch processing, caching, and data compression to minimize latency and maximize throughput while considering regional access patterns and cost constraints. The platform can also implement load balancing and failover mechanisms to ensure that time-sensitive data is processed first and reduce the impact of any single point of failure.

To evaluate the impact of each architectural decision on system stability, cost-effectiveness, and scalability, the platform can be tested using a comprehensive test suite that verifies the correctness, performance, and resilience of the system under various stress conditions, including high concurrency and network latency. The test suite can include scenarios such as simulated data ingestion, processing, and storage loads, as well as failure scenarios to test the platform's ability to recover from unexpected errors or failures.

To incorporate real-world constraints, such as varying network conditions, peak loads, and diverse tenant configurations, into the design, the platform can be designed to handle dynamic dependencies and external data sources effectively, handling changes and ensuring consistent performance. The platform can also be designed with a modular code structure and clear documentation to facilitate future enhancements and debugging."]

