Here is an example of how you could implement the semaphore management system in C:
```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>

// Define the number of concurrent threads and the initial value for the semaphore
#define NUM_THREADS 20
#define INITIAL_VALUE 50

// Structure to hold information about each thread
typedef struct {
    pthread_t thread;
    int id;
} thread_info_t;

// Global variables for the semaphore and threads
semaphore_t *sem = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
thread_info_t threads[NUM_THREADS];
int num_threads = 0;

// Function to initialize the semaphore and start the threads
void init() {
    // Initialize the semaphore with the initial value
    sem = sem_open("sem", O_CREAT | O_EXCL, 0644, INITIAL_VALUE);
    if (sem == NULL) {
        perror("Error creating semaphore");
        exit(1);
    }

    // Create and start the threads
    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_THREADS; i++) {
        threads[i].id = i;
        pthread_create(&threads[i].thread, NULL, thread_func, &threads[i]);
    }
}

// Function to acquire the semaphore and perform a blocking operation
void block() {
    // Acquire the semaphore with a timeout of 1 second
    if (sem_wait(sem, 1000) == -1) {
        perror("Error waiting for semaphore");
        exit(1);
    }
}

// Function to acquire the semaphore and perform a non-blocking operation
void nonblock() {
    // Acquire the semaphore with a timeout of 0 seconds (non-blocking)
    if (sem_trywait(sem, 0) == -1) {
        perror("Error trying to acquire semaphore");
        exit(1);
    }
}

// Function to release the semaphore
void release() {
    // Release the semaphore
    if (sem_post(sem) == -1) {
        perror("Error releasing semaphore");
        exit(1);
    }
}

// Function to handle errors and print diagnostics
void error_handler(const char *msg, int errcode) {
    printf("%s: %s\n", msg, strerror(errcode));
}

// Function to handle concurrent access to the semaphore
void* thread_func(void *arg) {
    // Get the thread ID and increment the number of threads
    thread_info_t *thread = arg;
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    num_threads++;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);

    // Perform the operation with the semaphore
    switch (thread->id % 3) {
        case 0:
            block();
            printf("Thread %d acquired the semaphore\n", thread->id);
            sleep(1);
            release();
            break;
        case 1:
            nonblock();
            if (sem_trywait(sem, 0) == -1) {
                error_handler("Error trying to acquire semaphore", errno);
            } else {
                printf("Thread %d acquired the semaphore\n", thread->id);
                sleep(1);
                release();
            }
            break;
        case 2:
            block();
            printf("Thread %d acquired the semaphore\n", thread->id);
            sleep(1);
            release();
            break;
    }

    // Decrement the number of threads and return
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    num_threads--;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    return NULL;
}

// Main function to run the program
int main() {
    // Initialize the semaphore and start the threads
    init();

    // Run the threads
    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_THREADS; i++) {
        pthread_join(threads[i].thread, NULL);
    }

    // Destroy the semaphore
    if (sem_close(sem) == -1) {
        perror("Error closing semaphore");
        exit(1);
    }

    return 0;
}
```
This implementation uses a semaphore to control access to a shared resource, in this case the number of concurrent threads. The `block` and `nonblock` functions acquire the semaphore with different timeouts, causing one thread to block until it acquires the semaphore and another to continue without waiting. The `release` function releases the semaphore when it is no longer needed.

The `thread_func` function is called by each thread and performs the operation with the semaphore. It uses a switch statement to determine which operation to perform based on the thread's ID. If the thread fails to acquire the semaphore, it prints an error message and exits. Otherwise, it acquires the semaphore and continues with the operation.

The `main` function initializes the semaphore and starts the threads using the `pthread_create` function. It then joins each thread using the `pthread_join` function to ensure they have completed their operations before continuing. Finally, it destroys the semaphore using the `sem_close` function.

This implementation handles overflow and underflow conditions by checking for errors when acquiring or releasing the semaphore. It also provides clear diagnostics for troubleshooting by printing error messages if an error occurs.

To handle transient network failures, you could use a distributed key-value store like Redis to persistently store state across multiple regions. You can then use mechanisms like reconnection and state reconciliation to ensure that the system remains available even in the presence of failed processes or network partitions.

To optimize for low latency and high throughput under high concurrency, you could use a message queue like RabbitMQ to buffer operations until they can be executed by the semaphore. This would allow multiple threads to enqueue their operations simultaneously without having to compete for the semaphore.

To handle unpredictable load spikes, you could use techniques like burst buffering or dynamic scaling to adjust the size of the semaphore based on the current load. This would allow the system to scale up or down depending on the available resources and the workload.']

